#X obj 1432 408 + -1;
#X text 1171 403 So \, setting a creation argument of;
#X text 1175 434 -1 in [+] allows the count to start;
#X text 1181 463 at 0 And if you stop and start it;
#X text 1173 497 again without a reset \, it just continues;
#X text 129 16 >1) Using the logic operators \, you can cause oscillators to turn on and off. A sequncer turns oscillators on and off in an order that follows a number. To start simply but still follow what might be considered the simplest but requires thought \, a monosynth with only two oscillators is where to start this patch. -------------------------------------->;
#X text 574 21 >2) A sequencer has a series of steps. An 8 step sequencer is often a good place to start and that is often what you see in eurorack modules as well as a number of other hardware synths. Instead of a [random] though \, we need something that counts. I have seen a counting mechanism in Pd before. There is a good place to start thinking. ---------------------->;
#X text 1033 23 >3) A counting mechanism has to increase by +1 each time. And I remember that the one type I have seen goes back up to itsef. I am trying to work it out without having to look for it. ---------------------------------------------------->;
#X text 1076 130 >4) This mechanism to the right works but it starts at 2 and counts up by 1 I need it to start at 1 Eventually \, it will have to return to 1 after it counts to 8 Start to the left side of the window. To continue. <----------------------;
#X text 15 181 >5) That was simple. Just remove the 1 creation argument from the [+]. Now I need to reset the counter after it reaches 8 -------------------------------------------------------->;
#X text 478 170 >6) One way to get around having to reset the number is to look up how to create a modulo operation. Then I could just use the modulo operation to count using the remainder of numbers divided by 8 However \, if you leave the counter going long enough \, the count might eventually run out before the patch was closed. I will just have to work through the mechanism. Maybe I can still use modulo.;
#X text 683 322 >7) This is usually the symbol used in programming to represent the modulo operator in math. However \, it is currently deprecated in Pd \, it being version version Pd 0.55.2 on March 7 \, 2026 \, that I am using. The current modulo operator is [mod].;
#X text 737 453 >8) This mechanism to the left works as expected. On with more. ------------------------------------------------------->;
#X text 1156 300 >9) This is experimenting with the [+];
#X text 1175 517 on with the count. Continue to the left. <----------------------------------------------------;
#X text 35 549 >10) Just thinking about there being a creation argument with the [mod] and you can change that number using slider. This might allow you to change the number of steps in a step sequencer on the fly if you set it up properly. ------------------------------------------------------------ These logic operators can act as if statements or at lease conditional statements in type programming \, I believe.;
